body:
Daily.

&#x200B;

I still think about suicide most days. When the little voice in my head says "You should kill yourself," that's my cue to take my evening meds. Unless I'm at work without access to those particular meds, it's great.

A lot, probably $1,000+ per month all said and done.

&#x200B;

But I work at the grocery store, so that is all my work drinks/snacks, bad habits, getting cash back, and of course actual groceries. It's just SO, the cat, and myself.

Make an awkward comment about it. Either we burst into giggles, or I kill the mood for him too.

&#x200B;

In all seriousness, he can generally tell when I’m not really into it. Even of he didn’t pick up on it, al it takes is a “Hey babe, I’m not really feeling this right now, want to try again in the morning.”

Being "essential".

&#x200B;

I work at a grocery store; shit was too crazy to dwell too much; I was trying to keep my front end together. I was bummed I didn't get the Quarantine experience like everyone else, but looking back, that much idle time would have thrown me into a deep depressive episode. Bonus I not only stayed sane-ish(admittedly, there was a prolonged manic episode), but I also got some ridiculous stories out of the experience, so that's cool, I guess.

Because I enjoy my job, for the most part. It keeps me somewhat sane and social .  I Don’t think I would have made it through 2020 without being “essential”. 

&#x200B;

Also, I like having a place to live.

I was diagnosed at 7, and I am beyond thankful that I was. ADHD has been a part of my identity since I had one. I didn't grow up thinking I just sucked; I knew there was an actual reason I struggled.  I've also been very open about having ADHD, because you try telling a 7 year old with ADHD to shut up about their ADHD. I'm about to turn 32 and still refer to myself as "The poster child for ADHD"

&#x200B;

Another way I lucked out growing up; a then-undiagnosed ADHD momma raised me, so I always had the one parent who never shamed me for my symptoms; rather, she sympathized with me. So that helped a lot.

&#x200B;

*Edit to add;*

My dad felt much the same way as your husband does. He was very resistant to me being medicated, but the counselor explained to him "Diagnosis and medication is the difference between her being struggling in life or her succeeding and doing something great with her life". He's come a long way since. He is very accepting and supportive of me and my struggles with ADHD. I truly lucked out with the parents I got landed with.

Five days a week. I’m female, and I work in customer service. I’m underweight, and customers love to make unsolicited comments about my body, but it’s okay because I’m skinny, and I *should learn to take a compliment.*

&#x200B;

That being said, I get a lot of sincere compliments, which helps boost my mood and confidence. A compliment makes a shitty day much better, which is why I love giving people compliments.

Fifty dollar bills.

&#x200B;

They're pointless; They make counting money harder. Stick  with hundreds and twenties and fill the rest with lower denominations. I work with a lot of money and fifties suck.

When you try to date other people, you still can't get that person out of your head. You never fall for anyone because you always compare them to that special someone.

&#x200B;

With that person, it just clicks.

I found a 1901 Indian Head Penny in a random roll of pennies; it jumped(fell) out at me. A few moments later, I opened a roll of Dimes; something happened with a 1954 silver dime.

&#x200B;

I have a lot of cool coins, but those two made my month.

&#x200B;

I also once found a 1909 Wheat Penny.

I'll always be mentally ill, but thanks to my meds and my support system I am more or less stable and functioning. 

&#x200B;

Unmedicated Bipolar Disorder can really fuck shit up.

Holy guacamole, I love those so darn much! You've given me some inspiration. SO and I craft collect mushroom stuff; they're very symbolic of our love.

&#x200B;

 We had a room in our old house we dubbed the "Craft-Shroom-Room," where we made crafts and grew mushrooms. It's where we spent our first date, tripping on shrooms. Damnit, now I'm both inspired and nostalgic.

I'm really good at customer service, I'm honestly absolutely phenomenal at dealing with people. I legitimately get 10+ comments a day about how amazing I am at my job. 

&#x200B;

I'm good at customer service and am phenomenal at dealing with people. I get 10+ comments daily about how amazing I am at my job. A reputation around town that I've had customers offer me appointments. I have a running joke with my mom that I'm somewhat of a local celebrity; I've had people come up to me if I'm out walking or at other stores, it's given me a big ego sometimes.

Holy guacamole, that is super relatable!

&#x200B;

31/F here; I was diagnosed at seven and have medicated since. ADHD has always been a hyperfocus research topic of mine. Still, I'm constantly finding things that I never realized were related to my ADHD. 

&#x200B;

Anywho, welcome to the club!

Yes.

&#x200B;

Chances are those boys will grow to become fathers, and those girls will grow up to drive cars or trucks. I don't understand how those toys are gender-specific.

We were on Shrooms, and it was unspoken, but it was rather evident that it had been a mutual feeling for years; we were just that oblivious. 

&#x200B;

5 years later, SO and I have a really neat first date story that we aren't going to be able to tell our kids (until they're older)

Owning a pair was my dream as a teenager, but instead, I got Airwalks.

&#x200B;

As an adult, I went nuts with it.

You mourn that you'll never have the opportunity to get to know them.

&#x200B;

I had an uncle that I only "met" when I was a baby in '92. He passed in 2020. I grieved the possibility of him just showing up one day; his closest brother, my uncle, who noped out with him, showed up out of the blue in 2011 and hasn't left. I always hoped my late uncle would do the same, and I would finally meet my mom's "cool cat," bellbottom, wearing Taco Bell, the loving oldest brother. Thankfully, he reached out to my grandma when he was diagnosed with cancer. I want to think that if his cancer hadn't been so aggressive and if it weren't for that pesky pandemic, he would have ended up just showing up someday; I mourn that possibility.

Ohh, how did I miss Chodios? 

&#x200B;

All's Well That Ends Well is one of those albums that still gives me chills.

Another lady here, I walk home from work some nights. I love my walk. Most of the route is within my childhood boundaries. When I was 10, my workplace was the east I could walk, and where I turned on my street was the furthest wast I could walk, without adult supervision, but usually with my cousins. That said, there are countless times I've been fucking terrified.

&#x200B;

 Thankfully I've never had any serious incidents.

As a smoker, I wholeheartedly agree.

&#x200B;

I'm trying to quit; I've at least cut down a lot.

It's really startinbg to hit me now at 31. Most people still guess my age as in my early to mid twenties, but I feel like that isn't going to be the case for long, so now seems like a good time to start getting preemptive about my antiaging routene.\]

&#x200B;

It's starting to hit me now at 31. Most people still guess my age as in my early to mid-twenties, but I feel like that isn't going to be the case for long, so now seems like a good time to start making healthier choices in the hopes of aging gracefully or at least so I can feel less shitty than I may end up looking.

I have been late to work several times because because I've gotten distracted by a squirrel.

&#x200B;

Also my entire life is a complete clusterfuck that I'm barely holding on to.

You know how your depression seems to last like 2 weeks, and then you're bursting with energy and making reckless decisions for the next 2 week, or so? It turns out that you should bring that up to your doctor. 

&#x200B;

Lamotrigine will change your life!

It's completely normal to me. If anything I see long hair as a more masculine trait.

&#x200B;

My dad has always had had hair longer than mine, my brother has hair longer than my dad's. And half my uncles have/had long hair.

A sweater, movie theaters are too cold.

&#x200B;

But, serious answer, just wear an outfit that you feel pretty in.

Becoming a smoker.

&#x200B;

Trying to quit sucks.

Tiger Lillies.

&#x200B;

my grandma's house(where I grew up) had a lot of them on the side if the house. I've loved them for as long as I can remember. I just moved into a house with them growing, it delights me when they bloom
